From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 23:41:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 21/37] LLHD Deseq: register/probe/instance results are free
 inputs, not poison

computeBoolean's fallback poisons any unsupported op with a
trigger-structured i1 operand -- and chased THROUGH state elements into
their input cones to decide it. A register result at process-evaluation
time does not combinationally depend on the current trigger event, no
matter what its next/reset wiring mentions: it is some register's current
state, i.e. a free input, exactly like a module port. Return unknown for
seq.firreg, seq.compreg, llhd.prb and hw.instance results instead of
recursing into them.

The found case is OpenC910's ct_ebiu_write_channel, the last unlowered
module in any of the three test designs: an earlier process in the module
had already lowered to a toggle register
    %aw_vb_sel = seq.firreg (xor %en, %aw_vb_sel) ... reset (xor %rst, 1)
and a later process's drive enable reads it; the reset wiring mentions the
analyzed process's own reset trigger, so the whole module was refused as
'poison condition'.

Also names the poisoning op under LLVM_DEBUG -- the abort was previously
undebuggable ('Condition: x' says nothing about WHERE the analysis gave up),
and finding this took exactly one run once the print existed.

OpenC910: residual llhd 11 -> 0, modules holding llhd 1 -> 0 of 504,
registers 4,787 -> 4,789 (the two recovered processes' state). CVA6
unchanged (301 registers, 0 llhd). Reproducer (a process whose enable reads
another process's toggle register) lowers to 0 llhd and is SAT-proven
equivalent to its RTL at d8.
---
 lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp
index d85d1d0ec..c922417cd 100644
--- a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp
+++ b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Deseq.cpp
@@ -765,6 +765,20 @@ TruthTable Deseq::computeBoolean(OpResult value) {
     return result;
   }
 
+  // Results of state elements and other opaque defs -- registers, signal
+  // probes, instance outputs -- are FREE INPUTS to this analysis: their value
+  // at process-evaluation time does not combinationally depend on the current
+  // trigger event, even when their own input cone mentions the trigger
+  // signals. The generic fallback below would chase THROUGH them into that
+  // cone and report poison. OpenC910's ct_ebiu_write_channel is the found
+  // case: an earlier process in the same module has already been lowered to
+  //   %aw_vb_sel = seq.firreg (xor %en, %aw_vb_sel) ... reset (xor %rst, 1)
+  // and a later process's drive enable reads %aw_vb_sel -- the reset wiring
+  // mentions the analyzed process's own reset trigger, so the whole module
+  // was refused over a value that is simply "some register's current state".
+  if (isa<seq::FirRegOp, seq::CompRegOp, llhd::ProbeOp, hw::InstanceOp>(op))
+    return getUnknownBoolean();
+
   // Otherwise check if the operation depends on any of the triggers. If it
   // does, create a poison value since we don't really know how the trigger
   // affects this boolean. If it doesn't, create an unknown value.
@@ -776,8 +790,11 @@ TruthTable Deseq::computeBoolean(OpResult value) {
         auto result = computeBoolean(operand);
         return result.isPoison() || (result != getUnknownBoolean() &&
                                      !result.isTrue() && !result.isFalse());
-      }))
+      })) {
+    LLVM_DEBUG(llvm::dbgs()
+               << "  - Poison boolean at unsupported op: " << *op << "\n");
     return getPoisonBoolean();
+  }
   return getUnknownBoolean();
 }
 
